From 7ac4fac41194690ef5e8cfff931b343860c1ff69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Clio Brando <32285172+cliobrando@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:03:37 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] video: decode H.264 on the Rockchip VPU through MPP, VLC as
 fallback

Game previews and the video screensaver are 640x480 H.264. libVLC
decodes them in software: ~110 % of a core on the R36 Ultra, about
150 % for ES in total while the random-video screensaver runs.

MppVideoPlayer is a second playback backend inside VideoVlcComponent:
libavformat demuxes, MPP decodes on the VPU (VDPU2 via
/dev/mpp_service), libswscale converts NV12 to RGBA into the same
double-buffered VideoContext surfaces the VLC callbacks fill, and the
audio track is decoded with libavcodec and queued on its own SDL audio
device. Rendering, theme effects, fit/stretch sizing, looping,
playlists and the 0.7 s playlist start offset stay in
VideoVlcComponent and are shared by both backends.

ffmpeg and librockchip_mpp are loaded with dlopen when the first video
opens, and only if /dev/mpp_service is accessible; any missing library
or symbol, another codec, or an MPP init failure plays the video
through VLC exactly as before. Linking them instead made ES load 174
shared libraries instead of 81 and stall for about a minute on a cold
power-on.

New setting VideoDecoder: "auto" (default, MPP when possible) or
"vlc". Build with -DENABLE_MPP_VIDEO=ON (default OFF) and
ES_VIDEO_DEPS_INCLUDE pointing at the libav*/libsw* and rockchip/
headers; nothing changes for builds without it.

Measured on the R36 Ultra (6.1 kernel): random-video screensaver
ES ~150 % -> ~70 % of a core, the MPP decode thread itself ~4 %;
cold start to main() unchanged (~0.45 s, 81 libraries). A standalone
MPP decode of a 30 s preview took 1.8 s of CPU against 12.1 s for
libavcodec, with bit-identical frames.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
